      "feedback": "Here is a critical, fact-based review of your preview page. The draft is already in great shape structurally, but a few subtle factual tweaks and tighter language will make it airtight and professional for the general public.\n\nFactual Corrections & Tweaks\nWhen to See It (Months): The draft currently says they are present from October through March. In Southwest Florida, Yellow-bellied Sapsuckers regularly stay well into April before migrating back north. Extending the window to October through April is more accurate for the park.\n\nThe \"Completely Migratory\" Nuance: Calling it the only woodpecker in eastern North America that is \"completely migratory\" is a tiny bit misleading. A small handful of hardy individuals do linger in northern states during mild winters. It is more accurate to describe them as the only eastern woodpecker that is a true long-distance migrant, with a distinct and massive shift between wintering and breeding ranges.\n\nThe \"Yellow-Bellied\" Field Mark: While the text ",
